Micro-benchmarks for the interpreter and the threaded runtime.

These are not part of "make Test": timings depend on the machine and vary
from run to run.  Each benchmark runs in its own process; runperf runs
each one several times and reports the median of every measurement.

   make                         run every benchmark
   make RUNPERF="-r 3 -s 0.5"   fewer runs, half the work
   ./runperf call deref         run only some benchmarks

To compare two builds, either save and compare results:

   make Save OUT=before.txt     (with the old build)
   make Compare BASE=before.txt (with the new build)

or run both interpreters in turn on every repetition, which is fairer on
a busy machine:

   ./runperf -x /old/bin/iconx -x /new/bin/iconx

Benchmarks:

   call           procedure call and return
   deref          reading locals, globals, record fields and list elements
   intarith       small-integer arithmetic
   realarith      real arithmetic
   strings        concatenation and string scanning
   records        record allocation
   lists          list allocation
   tables         table allocation
   queens         generators, list subscripts and calls (11 queens)
   queens-mt      the same, after a thread has run in the process
   msg            blocking messages from one thread to another
   scale-*        the same work in one thread, then in each of two threads;
                  -speedup is 2 * (one thread) / (two threads), 2.0 ideal
